%[text] %[text:anchor:T_6C25F7E4] # Segmentation with MATLAB Apps
%[text] The common workflow for image processing is to open an image, enhance that image, and then segment that image. **Segmentation** means separating the pixels you care about from everything else. The result is a **mask**: a logical array that is TRUE at every pixel belonging to your object, and FALSE everywhere else.
%[text] In this exercise we segment images two different ways, using two built-in MATLAB apps: the **Color Thresholder** (segmenting by color) and the **Image Segmenter** (segmenting with a machine learning model). We then measure how good each mask is, and finish by separating and counting objects that are touching each other.
%[text] **Before class:** the Segment Anything section needs a one-time download that can take several minutes. If you can, open the Image Segmenter App and click **Segment Anything** ahead of time, so the download is already done. 
%[text] 
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ## Example 1: The Cube
%[text] For our first example, we are going to segment those pesky tiles that appear to have different colors.
clearvars
close all

mmSetUnitDataFolder(2) % change folder to unit2 %[output:798f917e]
cube = imread("cube-illusion-beau-lotto.png");
imshow(cube) %[output:4405809a]
%[text] 
%[text] ### Color Thresholder
%[text] The Color Thresholder App is a useful way to explore color models. Note the naming: the app is called **Color Thresholder** in the Apps gallery, but the function that launches it is `colorThresholder` - one word, no space.
%[text] 1. Find the Color Thresholder App in the Apps tab, under the Image Processing and Computer Vision section.
%[text] 2. Click on the star to favorite the app, so it is easier to find next time.
%[text] 3. Select the app to open it.
%[text] 4. Hold down the Load Image menu button and select "Load Image from Workspace".
%[text] 5. Select the `cube` variable. \
%[text] Select the RGB color model.
%[text] 1. Drag the channel sliders.
%[text] 2. Rotate the color cube around - notice how the colors are distributed.
%[text] 3. Use the lasso tool to select the brown tile. This selects every pixel with a matching color.
%[text] 4. Can you isolate just the brown tiles? \
%[text] Click on the "New Color Space" button and select HSV.
%[text] - Notice how the sliders have changed: all of the colors are now in the circular controller.
%[text] - Rotate the 3D color cube around.
%[text] - Try to lasso the brown tile. \
%[text] Click "New Color Space" one more time and select L\*a\*b\*.
%[text] - What is similar?
%[text] - What is different? \
%[text] **The point of this example:** no matter which color model you pick, you cannot separate the "brown" tiles from the "orange" ones - they have nearly identical pixel values. They only *look* different to you because your visual system is correcting for the shadow falling across the cube. The thresholding tools only see the numbers, and the numbers are the same. Keep this in mind for the rest of the unit: what looks obviously separable to your eye is not always separable in the pixel data. \\
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_37B586C1] ## Example 2: The Strawberry
%[text] For this example, we want to capture and count the strawberries.
clear cube % clear the cube image, but keep any masks you have already exported
colorThresholder close % close the previous App

strawberry = imread("strawberries.png"); % read the image into the workspace
imshow(strawberry) %[output:5bacfc8a]
%[text] - Which color model should we use?
%[text] - The reds are so red - maybe HSV? \
%%
%[text] ### Prototyping Color Segmentation
%[text] We'll use the Color Thresholder to prototype our approach. Prototyping means working the segmentation out by hand, with sliders, before we commit it to code.
colorThresholder(strawberry) % launch the app and load the strawberry image
%[text] Try all three color models (RGB, HSV, and L\*a\*b\*).
%[text] - Use the lasso tool to capture the strawberries.
%[text] - Adjust the background opacity so you can see the original image.
%[text] - Notice that we are using all three channels to segment the strawberries.
%[text] - You will also need to adjust the sliders manually to capture the full strawberry. A little noise is ok.
%[text] - Repeat for each color model and export all three masks, naming them `maskRGB`, `maskHSV`, and `maskLAB`. The rest of this exercise needs all three. \
%[text] ![](text:image:6daf)
%[text] ![](text:image:6141)
%[text] L\*a\*b\* capture example
%%
%[text] ### Backup masks
%[text] Didn't get a usable mask out of the App? No problem. I saved my own slider settings in the function `SegmentStrawberries`, at the very bottom of this script. Uncomment these three lines to use my masks instead, then carry on with the rest of the exercise.
% maskRGB = SegmentStrawberries(strawberry,'rgb');
% maskHSV = SegmentStrawberries(strawberry,'hsv');
% maskLAB = SegmentStrawberries(strawberry,'lab');
%%
%[text] ### Visualize results
%[text] A mask on its own is just a black and white image, which is hard to judge. A better way to look at one is to "burn" it into the original image, so you can see exactly which pixels were captured. The function `imoverlay` does this. We do it the long way once, below, and then use the course function `mmShowBurnImage` - which wraps `imoverlay` into a single step - for the rest of the exercise.
%[text] #### RGB Result
figure %[output:4c211f22]
burnedImage = imoverlay(strawberry,maskRGB); % combines mask and image into one
imshow(burnedImage) %[output:4c211f22]
%[text] - The RGB mask works, but it would need some serious morphological clean-up. \
%%
%[text] #### HSV Result
figure %[output:1d0463e9]
mmShowBurnImage(strawberry,maskHSV) % the course function, doing all three steps at once %[output:1d0463e9]
%[text] - better, but still noisy and missing some of the strawberry \
%[text] 
%%
%[text] #### L\*a\*b\* Result
figure %[output:8ca90b62]
mmShowBurnImage(strawberry,maskLAB) %[output:8ca90b62]
%[text] - Way better - it captures most of the strawberries with just a little noise. \
%%
%[text] ### Roll your own Segmentation Function
%[text] If you are going to threshold the image or similar images, repeatedly, it is easy to create a function using the Color Thresholder.  This function captures (and stores) the exact slider positions for each channel you set. It then uses those slider values for a series of logical operations that creates the mask,  e.g. "set all pixels with intensities that fall inside the slider ranges to TRUE"
%[text] To create the function, switch back to the Color Thresholder App and then:
%[text] 1. Hold down the Export button.
%[text] 2. Choose "Export Function" \
%[text] The function will open in a new editor tab. Review it, then copy it and paste it into the **Back up Functions** section at the very bottom of this script. MATLAB will let you paste a function almost anywhere in a live script, but keeping all of your functions together at the bottom makes the script much easier to read.

%[text] - Or just review my version of the function below. \
%%
%[text] Here's my function from a previous L\*a\*b\* segmentation. I renamed the function `createLABmask` (to differentiate it from yours). I also cropped out some of the comments for compactness, and added two morphological operation steps to clean up the noise.
%[text] Notice how this function accepts an `RGB` image and returns both a mask (`BW`) and something called `maskedRGBImage`. `BW` is what we created and saved in the workspace. The `maskedRGBImage` is an RGB image where only the pixels inside the mask (the strawberries) are displayed in color. The rest of the image is set to black.
%[text] Also notice what the function does. It converts the RGB image into L\*a\*b\* space with `rgb2lab`, and then builds the mask with one big logical test: a pixel is set to TRUE only if it falls inside the slider range on **all three** channels at once. Finally, it cleans up the result with `bwareaopen` and `imfill`.
%[text] Let's try the function I saved.
[maskLAB2, maskedRGBImage] = createLABmask(strawberry);
figure; %[output:3d78d881]
imshowpair(maskLAB2, maskedRGBImage,'montage') %[output:3d78d881]
title('Mask | Masked RGB Image') %[output:3d78d881]
%[text] - I typically don't find the masked RGB image to be that useful, and usually ignore it. \
%[text]  
function [BW,maskedRGBImage] = createLABmask(RGB)
%createMask  Threshold RGB image using auto-generated code from colorThresholder app.
%------------------------------------------------------


% Convert RGB image to chosen color space
I = rgb2lab(RGB);

% Define thresholds for channel 1 based on histogram settings
channel1Min = 18.376;
channel1Max = 75.074;

% Define thresholds for channel 2 based on histogram settings
channel2Min = 16.027;
channel2Max = 71.004;

% Define thresholds for channel 3 based on histogram settings
channel3Min = 7.016;
channel3Max = 58.449;

% Create mask based on chosen histogram thresholds
sliderBW = (I(:,:,1) >= channel1Min ) & (I(:,:,1) <= channel1Max) & ...
    (I(:,:,2) >= channel2Min ) & (I(:,:,2) <= channel2Max) & ...
    (I(:,:,3) >= channel3Min ) & (I(:,:,3) <= channel3Max);
BW = sliderBW;

% Add MorphOps
BW = bwareaopen(BW,500); % remove noise
BW = imfill(BW,'holes'); % fill holes

% Initialize output masked image based on input image.
maskedRGBImage = RGB;

% Set background pixels where BW is false to zero.
maskedRGBImage(repmat(~BW,[1 1 3])) = 0;

end
%%
%[text] ## Segment Anything Model (SAM)
%[text] Ok, well that was fun. But what if you don't want to manually circle strawberries? Well, that's where [SAM](https://github.com/facebookresearch/segment-anything) comes into play. Brought to you by the same people who brought you Facebook and the Meta Glasses, SAM is a machine learning model trained on 11 million images and 1.1 billion masks. It was designed to recognize objects in an image. Let's give it a whirl.
%[text] You can find SAM in the Image Segmenter App (not the Color Thresholder).
imageSegmenter(strawberry)
%[text] **Click on the Segment Anything button.**
%[text] - The first time you do this, the App will download the SAM network.
%[text] - This can take several minutes, so be patient. \
%[text] **Using Segment Anything Model (SAM)**:
%[text] Once the panel comes up:
%[text] 1. Select the SAM option and segment the strawberries by clicking on them (marking them as "Foreground"). You will need to click on several strawberries before you capture them all.
%[text] 2. Remove any incorrect mask by marking that area as "Background".
%[text] 3. **Export the Code**: You can export the code used for segmentation by clicking on the appropriate option in the app. Review the code to get a sense of what is going on.
%[text] 4. **Export the Mask** as `maskSAM`. \
%[text] If SAM will not run on your machine, use my saved mask instead:
% load('strawberry-maskSAM.mat','maskSAM')
%%
%[text] ### Review SAM Mask
figure
mmShowBurnImage(strawberry,maskSAM) %[output:2007ce30]
%%
%[text] ## Comparing Masks
%[text] So which is the best mask? Ideally, for comparison, you should have a ground truth image where a human has gone through and manually segmented the strawberries. I have lovingly and painstakingly created such a mask. Let's load it and visualize it now:
figure; %[output:90bf8af6]
load('strawberry-mask-ground-truth.mat','maskGT'); % loads as maskGT
mmShowBurnImage(strawberry,maskGT) %[output:90bf8af6]
title('Ground Truth') %[output:90bf8af6]
%[text] - As you can see, perfection.  \
%%
%[text] ### Overlay Masks
%[text] We can visualize the mask differences using `imshowpair`.
p = struct('maskRGB',maskRGB,'maskHSV',maskHSV,'maskLAB',maskLAB,'maskSAM',maskSAM,'maskGT',maskGT); % create a structure with all the masks
fns = string(fieldnames(p));
fns(fns == "maskGT") = []; % remove the ground truth - we compare everything else against it

figure; %[output:3d8bdb3c]
tiledlayout("horizontal","TileSpacing",'none','Padding','tight') %[output:3d8bdb3c]

numFN = numel(fns); % number of fields to evaluate
for n=1:numFN
    nexttile %[output:3d8bdb3c]
    imshowpair(p.maskGT, p.(fns(n)));
    title(fns(n))
end

sgtitle('Magenta - Comp only, Green - GT only, White - overlap','FontSize',12) %[output:3d8bdb3c]
%[text] In these pseudocolored images:
%[text] - Mask that is found in the comparison mask, but not in the GT, is colored magenta
%[text] - Mask that is found in the GT but not the comp map is colored green
%[text] - Mask that overlaps is white \
%[text] You can see that the mask gets better as we move from RGB to SAM - less magenta, less green, more white.
%%
%[text] ### Quantifying the mask differences
%[text] There are two calculations commonly used to compare a mask against a ground truth: the **Jaccard Similarity Coefficient** and the **Dice Coefficient**.
%[text] - The **Jaccard index** is the size of the intersection divided by the size of the union (MASK AND divided by MASK OR). This is also called **intersection over union** (**IoU**).
%[text] - The **Dice coefficient** counts the overlap twice: two times the intersection, divided by the sum of the two mask areas. Because of that, Dice always returns a slightly higher score than Jaccard for the same pair of masks. \
%[text] ![](text:image:2209)
%[text] Both functions return a number between 0 and 1, where a higher value means the two masks are more similar.
%[text] These functions are easy to use. You just pass in the two masks that you want to compare.
jaccard(maskGT,maskRGB)  %[output:7c8f6ee5]
dice(maskGT,maskRGB) %[output:9d4a41a8]
%[text] - Notice that Jaccard returns the lower of the two numbers. It is the more restrictive measure. \
%%
%[text] We can upgrade our previous for loop to calculate both coefficients, display the overlay, and collate the results in a table.
figure %[output:23648c34]
tiledlayout("horizontal","TileSpacing",'none','Padding','tight') %[output:23648c34]

T = table(strings(numFN,1), zeros(numFN,1), zeros(numFN,1), ...
    'VariableNames',["Mask" "JC" "Dice"]); % preallocate table

for n=1:numFN %[output:group:5b99f15b]
    nexttile %[output:23648c34]
    imshowpair(p.maskGT, p.(fns(n))); % display the two masks
    
    jc = jaccard(p.maskGT, p.(fns(n))); % calculate the jaccard coefficient
    dc = dice(p.maskGT, p.(fns(n))); % calculate the dice coefficient
    
    title(sprintf('%s: %1.2f, %1.2f',fns(n),jc,dc)) % title includes calculated coefficients

    % add coefficients to a table
    T.Mask(n) = fns(n);
    T.JC(n) = jc;
    T.Dice(n) = dc;
end %[output:group:5b99f15b]
T %[output:2dd82dfb]
%[text] - As you can see, the coefficients increase as you go down the table: RGB is the worst match to the ground truth, and SAM is the best. \
%%
%[text] ## Separate Touching Strawberries 
%[text] If we try to count the strawberries now using MATLAB, we would get a count that is far fewer than the actual number of strawberries. This is because many of the  strawberry masks are fused. This often happens in segmentation when objects are too close together. Luckily, we have the watershed transformation.
%[text] The watershed transform can detach connected components (CC) that are just barely touching. Its name comes from the geological concept of a watershed - a region of relatively high elevation separating two lower lying regions (or basins).
%[text] ![](text:image:1318)![](text:image:0b66)
%%
%[text] ### Clean the mask
%[text] It's a good idea to clean up any noise in a mask before separating the touching strawberries, because the watershed transform will happily treat a speck of noise as its own object. Here we use three morphological operations:
p.maskSAMclean = bwareaopen(p.maskSAM,50); % remove small noise
p.maskSAMclean = imfill(p.maskSAMclean,'holes'); % fill any holes inside the strawberries
p.maskSAMclean = imclearborder(p.maskSAMclean); % remove objects touching the image border
figure %[output:5e7eeb58]
mmShowBurnImage(strawberry,p.maskSAMclean) %[output:5e7eeb58]
%[text] - Notice that `imclearborder` removed the small strawberry in the corner, because it runs off the edge of the image. This is standard practice when you are **measuring** objects: a strawberry that is half out of frame would give you a bad area measurement. But notice the trade-off - if you are **counting**, you just threw away a real strawberry. \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BA36DD21] ### mmGetWatershed
%[text] The course function **`mmGetWatershed`** automates the process of separating touching blobs. 
%[text] Here we use the following inputs:
%[text] 1. The mask
%[text] 2. The size of the regional minima (part of the watershed process).
%[text] 3. ShowSteps = true - visualizes the steps and the final outcome. \
p.watershed = mmGetWatershed(p.maskSAMclean,5,"ShowSteps",true); %[output:81eee895]
%[text] - and that works pretty well.
%[text] - What happens if you change Input 2 to a 1? Try it, and determinet whether you end up with too many pieces or too few. \
%%
%[text] ### Review the final product
figure; %[output:927247f9]
mmShowBurnImage(strawberry,p.watershed); %[output:927247f9]
%[text] - Pretty, pretty, good.  \
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ### Count the strawberries
%[text] Separating the touching blobs was the whole point, so now we can finally count them by plugging the mask into region props: 
rp = regionprops('table',p.watershed,"basic") %[output:288aeb0a]
%[text] - How many strawberries did `regionprops` find? \
%%
%[text] ###  Review
%[text] Here's the original image. How many strawberries do you count?
figure;  %[output:7d0a2770]
imshow("strawberries.png") %[output:7d0a2770]
%%
%[text] And here's what region props found
imshow("strawberries.png") %[output:2eafbff3]
for n=1:height(rp) %[output:group:1317aa2a]
    centroid = rp.Centroid(n,:);
    text(centroid(1),centroid(2),sprintf('%d',n), ... %[output:2eafbff3]
        'Color','white','FontSize',14,'FontWeight','bold','HorizontalAlignment','center'); %[output:2eafbff3]
end %[output:group:1317aa2a]
%[text] 
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ## Back up Functions
function [BW] = SegmentStrawberries(RGB,colorModel)
% Converts Image to selected color model and then creates a mask based on
% the histogram slider settings for each channel

switch colorModel
    case 'rgb'
        % Convert RGB image to chosen color space
        I = RGB;

        % Define thresholds for channel 1 based on histogram settings
        channel1Min = 122.000;
        channel1Max = 255.000;

        % Define thresholds for channel 2 based on histogram settings
        channel2Min = 0.000;
        channel2Max = 130.000;

        % Define thresholds for channel 3 based on histogram settings
        channel3Min = 0.000;
        channel3Max = 168.000;

         % Create mask based on chosen histogram thresholds
        sliderBW = (I(:,:,1) >= channel1Min ) & (I(:,:,1) <= channel1Max) & ...
            (I(:,:,2) >= channel2Min ) & (I(:,:,2) <= channel2Max) & ...
            (I(:,:,3) >= channel3Min ) & (I(:,:,3) <= channel3Max);

    case 'hsv'
        % Convert RGB image to chosen color space
        I = rgb2hsv(RGB);

        % Define thresholds for channel 1 based on histogram settings
        channel1Min = 0.933;
        channel1Max = 0.048;

        % Define thresholds for channel 2 based on histogram settings
        channel2Min = 0.225;
        channel2Max = 1.000;

        % Define thresholds for channel 3 based on histogram settings
        channel3Min = 0.000;
        channel3Max = 1.000;

        % Create mask based on chosen histogram thresholds
        sliderBW = ( (I(:,:,1) >= channel1Min) | (I(:,:,1) <= channel1Max) ) & ...
            (I(:,:,2) >= channel2Min ) & (I(:,:,2) <= channel2Max) & ...
            (I(:,:,3) >= channel3Min ) & (I(:,:,3) <= channel3Max);
      
    case 'lab'
        % Convert RGB image to chosen color space
        I = rgb2lab(RGB);

        % Define thresholds for channel 1 based on histogram settings
        channel1Min = 20.206;
        channel1Max = 74.931;

        % Define thresholds for channel 2 based on histogram settings
        channel2Min = 16.586;
        channel2Max = 73.722;

        % Define thresholds for channel 3 based on histogram settings
        channel3Min = 8.345;
        channel3Max = 54.813;

        % Create mask based on chosen histogram thresholds
        sliderBW = (I(:,:,1) >= channel1Min ) & (I(:,:,1) <= channel1Max) & ...
            (I(:,:,2) >= channel2Min ) & (I(:,:,2) <= channel2Max) & ...
            (I(:,:,3) >= channel3Min ) & (I(:,:,3) <= channel3Max);
    otherwise
        disp('color model not found')
        return
end

BW = sliderBW;
BW = bwareaopen(BW,500); % remove noise
BW = imfill(BW,'holes');

end
%%
%[text] 

